  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2x-5}{x-4}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2x-5}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2y-5}{y-4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{2y-5}{y-4} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{2y-5}{y-4} \\\\ -x(y-4) & =2y-5 \\\\ -xy+4x & =2y-5 \\\\ 4x+5 & =xy+2y \\\\ 4x+5 & =y(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+5}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4x+5}{x+2}.\n$$",
